{
  "example": "In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`. {cite}`requirements.references.rust_item_selectors`",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment:** the example's scenario and outcomes are covered by the two tests that cite it.\n\n**What the example says.** In `src/lib.rs`, `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }` gives the selectors `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`.\n\n**Tests that cite it** (both carry `{test}` references to `requirements.references.selector_nested`):\n- `rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry` in `src/outline/tests.rs` (lines 35–100):\n  - It parses a source containing `fn outer() { fn inner() {} }`, using the path `src/lib.rs` exactly as the example does.\n  - It asserts that the list of all node selectors contains `fn[outer]/fn[inner]`.\n  - It asserts that `outline.resolve(\"fn[outer]/fn[inner]\")` returns a node whose `.selector` equals that string.\n  - The resolve step walks from the root and matches the segment `fn[outer]` among the root's children, then `fn[inner]` among that node's children. So `fn[outer]` must exist as a top-level node with that segment.\n- `selectors_name_syntactic_ancestry_and_duplicates` (lines 662–706) uses the same source line and asserts the same nested selector appears in the selector list. It uses the path `lib.rs`.\n\n**How the code supports the assertions.**\n- `Builder::walk` makes `inner` a child of the `outer` outline node, because a mapped `fn` node recurses with itself as the parent.\n- `disambiguate` in `src/outline/mod.rs` builds the selector as `parent.selector + \"/\" + segment`. A top-level node's selector is just its segment, so `fn[outer]` and `fn[outer]/fn[inner]` follow.\n\n**Minor point.** The two tests do not assert the bare `fn[outer]` string in a list. The `resolve` assertion and the `parent.selector/segment` construction make that selector's existence unavoidable. `line_queries_find_innermost_and_declared_nodes` (line 448) also asserts the literal selector `fn[outer]` for a source in `src/lib.rs` with a nested `inner`. That test is not tagged with this example. I judge the explicit-assertion gap too small to count against the example.\n\nAt least one `{test}` reference exists, and the setup and assertions cover the nested-function scenario and its expected selectors.",
  "references": "f3c49019e423d681f14511a10dd0ec04ee48b588885830e2e0fe64fed09ff45b",
